{"name":"Divine Pleasure","hex":"#f4efe1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"divine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a god.","Eternal, holy, or otherwise godlike."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One skilled in divinity; a theologian.","A minister of the gospel; a priest; a clergyman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/divine"},{"term":"pleasure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of being pleased or contented; gratification.","A person, thing or action that causes enjoyment."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["pleased to meet you, \"It's my pleasure\""]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pleasure"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pleasure","blocks":[{"text":"Pleasure is an experience that feels good, that involves the enjoyment of something. It contrasts with pain or suffering, which are forms of feeling bad. It is closely related to value, desire and action: humans and other conscious animals find pleasure enjoyable, positive or worthy of seeking. A great variety of activities may be experienced as pleasurable, such as eating, having sex, listening to music or playing games. Pleasure is part of various other mental states such as ecstasy, euphoria and flow. Happiness and well-being are closely related to pleasure but not identical with it. There is no general agreement as to whether pleasure should be understood as a sensation, a quality of experiences, an attitude to experiences or otherwise. Pleasure plays a central role in the family of philosophical theories known as hedonism."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleasure"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"divine":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/En-us-divine.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/1c/En-us-divine.ogg/En-us-divine.ogg.mp3"}],"pleasure":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/En-us-pleasure.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d6/En-us-pleasure.ogg/En-us-pleasure.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Strawberry on white background","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Strawberry_on_white_background.jpg/500px-Strawberry_on_white_background.jpg","ratio":1.618,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strawberry_on_white_background.jpg","share":0.705},{"title":"Peonies for pleasure (16209793339)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Peonies_for_pleasure_%2816209793339%29.jpg/500px-Peonies_for_pleasure_%2816209793339%29.jpg","ratio":0.6764380530973452,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peonies_for_pleasure_(16209793339).jpg","share":0.6225},{"title":"Ācārya Guņabhadra’s Ātmānuśāsana – Precept on the Soul","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/%C4%80c%C4%81rya_Gu%C5%86abhadra%E2%80%99s_%C4%80tm%C4%81nu%C5%9B%C4%81sana_%E2%80%93_Precept_on_the_Soul.jpg/500px-%C4%80c%C4%81rya_Gu%C5%86abhadra%E2%80%99s_%C4%80tm%C4%81nu%C5%9B%C4%81sana_%E2%80%93_Precept_on_the_Soul.jpg","ratio":0.6582456140350877,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%80c%C4%81rya_Gu%C5%86abhadra%E2%80%99s_%C4%80tm%C4%81nu%C5%9B%C4%81sana_%E2%80%93_Precept_on_the_Soul.jpg","share":0.54375},{"title":"Piece of chocolate cake on a white plate decorated with chocolate sauce","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Piece_of_chocolate_cake_on_a_white_plate_decorated_with_chocolate_sauce.jpg/500px-Piece_of_chocolate_cake_on_a_white_plate_decorated_with_chocolate_sauce.jpg","ratio":1.4699946893255444,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piece_of_chocolate_cake_on_a_white_plate_decorated_with_